          "text": "Sure \u2014 here\u2019s a reliable classic beef lasagne recipe (makes about 6\u20138 servings). Includes a meat ragu and a creamy b\u00e9chamel. Notes for vegetarian swap and make-ahead are at the end.\n\nIngredients\n- Olive oil, 1\u20132 tbsp\n- Onion, 1 medium, finely chopped\n- Garlic, 2\u20133 cloves, minced\n- Carrot, 1 small, finely diced (optional)\n- Celery stalk, 1, finely diced (optional)\n- Ground beef, 500 g (1 lb)\n- Red wine, 120 ml (1/2 cup) \u2014 optional\n- Canned crushed tomatoes or passata, 800 g (28 oz)\n- Tomato paste, 1\u20132 tbsp\n- Dried oregano or Italian herb mix, 1 tsp\n- Bay leaf, 1 (optional)\n- Salt and black pepper\n- Pinch of sugar (if tomatoes are tart)\n- Lasagne sheets, about 12 sheets (regular or no-boil; adjust quantity to your dish size)\n- Mozzarella, 200\u2013250 g (7\u20139 oz), shredded\n- Grated Parmesan or Pecorino, 60\u201380 g (1/2\u20133/4 cup)\n\nFor the b\u00e9chamel (white sauce)\n- Butter, 60 g (4 tbsp)\n- Plain/all-purpose flour, 60 g (about 1/2 cup)\n- Milk, 800 ml (3 1/4 cups)\n- Salt, white or black pepper, and a pinch of grated nutmeg\n\nEquipment\n- 23 x 33 cm (9 x 13 in) baking dish (approx.) \u2014 adjust layers to your dish\n\nMethod\n1. Make the meat ragu\n   - Heat 1\u20132 tbsp olive oil in a large pan over medium heat. Saut\u00e9 onion (and carrot/celery if using) until softened, 5\u20137 minutes. Add garlic and cook 30 seconds.\n   - Add ground beef, break up with a spoon, and brown well. Season with salt and pepper.\n   - If using, pour in wine and simmer until reduced by half.\n   - Stir in crushed tomatoes, tomato paste, oregano, bay leaf and a pinch of sugar. Bring to a simmer, then lower heat and cook gently 20\u201330 minutes (longer if you have time) until thickened. Adjust seasoning; remove bay leaf.\n\n2. Make the b\u00e9chamel\n   - In a saucepan, melt butter over medium heat. Add flour and cook, stirring, 1\u20132 minutes to form a roux (do not brown).\n   - Gradually whisk in milk, a little at a time, then bring to a simmer, whisking until thickened and smooth (about 5\u20138 minutes). Season with salt, pepper and a pinch of nutmeg. Remove from heat.\n\n3. Assemble\n   - Preheat oven to 180\u00b0C (350\u00b0F).\n   - Spoon a thin layer of ragu across the bottom of the dish. Add a layer of lasagne sheets to cover (trim if needed).\n   - Spread more ragu over the sheets, then spoon/pour some b\u00e9chamel on top and sprinkle a little mozzarella and parmesan.\n   - Repeat: sheets \u2192 ragu \u2192 b\u00e9chamel \u2192 cheeses, making 2\u20133 layers. Finish with a top layer of b\u00e9chamel and a generous sprinkle of mozzarella and parmesan.\n\n4. Bake\n   - Cover loosely with foil (foil underside can be sprayed with oil or tent so it doesn\u2019t stick to cheese) and bake 25\u201330 minutes. Remove foil and bake another 10\u201315 minutes until bubbly and golden on top.\n   - Let rest 10\u201315 minutes before slicing so it sets and serves cleanly.\n\nTips and variations\n- No-boil lasagne sheets: use them as directed; ensure your ragu is not too dry (a bit more sauce helps) and don\u2019t pre-cook the sheets.\n- Vegetarian option: swap the beef for 300\u2013400 g cooked brown lentils or a mix of finely chopped mushrooms, spinach and diced roasted veg. Cook exactly like the ragu.\n- Make-ahead: assemble and refrigerate for up to 24 hours before baking. For longer storage, freeze unbaked lasagne (tightly wrapped) for up to 3 months; bake from frozen covered for ~60\u201375 minutes, then uncover and finish.\n- Cheese choices: fresh mozzarella for meltiness, mature parmesan for flavor. Ricotta can be layered in place of or in addition to b\u00e9chamel for a different texture.\n- To get crisp edges, bake uncovered for the last 10\u201315 minutes.\n\nTiming\n- Active prep: ~30\u201345 minutes\n- Simmer/assembly: ~30 minutes\n- Bake: ~35\u201345 minutes\n- Total: ~1.5\u20132 hours (including resting)\n\nIf you want, I can scale this to a different number of servings, convert measurements to all imperial or metric, or give a vegetarian or gluten-free variant. Which would you prefer?"
